// Namespaced-import example verified on morloc 0.109.0 (2026-10-23).

[[importing-modules]]
=== Importing modules

You have been importing modules since <<getting-started>>: `import root-py` to
get implementations, and `import .units-abstract` to use a file of your own.
This section covers the rest: what a module exports, how local paths resolve,
namespaced imports, and what your choice of `root-<lang>` costs.

==== Exports

A module declaration names the module and lists the terms other modules may
import from it. `module mylib (foo, bar)` exports `foo` and `bar` and nothing
else. To export everything the module defines, use the wildcard:

[source, morloc]
----
module mylib (*)
----

An empty list, `module myinstances ()`, exports no terms. Such a module is still
worth importing when it carries typeclass instances -- implementations of a
shared interface for one type, covered in <<typeclasses>>. Instances travel with
the module rather than the export list, so importing the module makes every
instance in it available; they cannot be picked individually.

A submodule that exists only to be imported by a parent may omit its name:

[source, morloc]
----
module (*)
----

Its name is then taken from the import path: if `main.loc` imports `.utils`, the
module in `utils.loc` (or `utils/main.loc`) is named `utils`.

A single file may contain more than one module. Each `module` keyword starts a
new one; everything indented under it, or appearing before the next `module`
keyword, belongs to it. A module body may also be empty:

[source, morloc]
----
module utils (helper)
import root-py
helper :: Int -> Int
helper x = x + 1

module main (run)
import utils (helper)
run :: Int -> Int
run x = helper x
----

==== Installed modules and `root`

An import without a leading dot names an installed module. `morloc make` fetches
a missing one, as you saw in <<abstract-modules>>; `morloc install root-py`
fetches it by hand. <<installing-modules>> covers installing in full.

`root` and a `root-<lang>` are not alternatives. `root` declares the standard
library -- the classes, and everything derivable from them -- and a
`root-<lang>` supplies the instances by sourcing native implementations. A
program needs both, so its import list names a `root-<lang>`.

Which one it names sets what a single invocation costs, because the program
starts that language's runtime before it can call anything:

[cols="1,1,3"]
|===
| Import | Per invocation | Where the time goes

| `root` alone, using only operations the nexus runs itself (<<intrinsics>>)
| about 8 ms
| the nexus process; no pool is started

| `root-cpp`
| about 21 ms
| a compiled pool; roughly 13 ms of that is starting the pool

| `root-py`
| about 132 ms
| a Python pool; starting the interpreter dominates
|===

Crossing into a pool is cheap and starting an interpreter is not. That rarely
matters for a batch job and decides how a frequently typed command feels.
Moving between backends is a one-line change to the import.

==== Selective imports

Without a selector, an import brings every exported term into scope. A
parenthesized list takes only the terms named:

[source, morloc]
----
import root-py             -- import everything from root-py
import .mylib              -- import everything from local mylib
import .mylib (foo, bar)   -- import only foo and bar from local mylib
----

==== Local module paths

When you write `import .foo`, the compiler looks for the module under the
root of the importing module's package. For your program, that is the
directory holding the entry file you passed to `morloc make`; for an installed
package, it is that package's own directory. It checks two locations, in order:

1. A file module: `foo.loc`
2. A directory module: `foo/main.loc`

Dot-separated paths map to nested directories. For example, `import .lib.math`
resolves to either `lib/math.loc` or `lib/math/main.loc`.

The package root is fixed for every file of a package, so a dotted import means
the same thing in every file of a project however deeply nested that file is,
and an installed package's local imports mean the same wherever it is imported. Paths in a
`source` declaration work the other way round: they resolve against the file
that names them, so a module and the native code it sources travel together
when either moves.

Here is an example project layout:

----
project/
  main.loc            -- module main, imports .utils and .lib.math
  utils.loc           -- module (*), a flat file module
  utils.py
  lib/
    math/
      main.loc        -- module (*), a directory module
      main.py
----

The top-level `main.loc` imports both:

[source, morloc]
----
module main (negate_square, square_negate)

type Py => Real = "float"

import .utils (negate)
import .lib.math (square)

negate_square :: Real -> Real
negate_square x = negate (square x)

square_negate :: Real -> Real
square_negate x = square (negate x)
----

The flat file `utils.loc` exports `negate`:

[source, morloc]
----
module (*)

source Py from "utils.py" ("negate")

type Py => Real = "float"

negate :: Real -> Real
----

And the directory module `lib/math/main.loc` exports `square`:

[source, morloc]
----
module (*)

source Py from "main.py" ("square")

type Py => Real = "float"

square :: Real -> Real
----

Local modules can also import other local modules, and the path is still written
from the project root. For example, if `bar/baz/main.loc` needs a module at
`bif/biz/`, it writes:

[source, morloc]
----
import .bif.biz (mul)
----

This resolves to `bif/biz.loc` or `bif/biz/main.loc` at the top of the project,
not to anything beneath `bar/baz/`. The identical line in the top-level
`main.loc` names the identical module.

Since `root` is also the name of a system module, a local directory named
`root/` must be imported with the dot prefix to avoid ambiguity:

[source, morloc]
----
import root         -- imports the system "root" module
import .root        -- imports the local "root/" directory
----

The dot prefix always forces local resolution, so there is never a collision
between local and system module names.

==== Namespaced imports

`import .feet as feet` brings a module's terms in under a prefix, so a call
says where its function lives and two modules may export the same name. Here
`feet.loc` and `inches.loc` each define `fromMeters`:

[source, morloc]
.feet.loc
----
module (*)

import root

fromMeters :: Real -> Real
fromMeters m = m * 3.28084
----

`inches.loc` is the same with `39.3701`. Both import cleanly side by side:

[source, morloc]
.lengths.loc
----
module lengths (toFeet, toInches)

import root-py
import .feet as feet
import .inches as inches

toFeet :: Real -> Real
toFeet = feet.fromMeters

toInches :: Real -> Real
toInches m = inches.fromMeters m
----

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make lengths.loc
$ ./lengths toFeet 2
6.56168
$ ./lengths toInches 2
78.7402
----

A namespaced term is reachable only through its prefix; a bare `fromMeters` in
`lengths.loc` is `Undefined term: fromMeters`. A selector still works after the
prefix, as in `import .feet as feet (fromMeters)`. Type names are never
qualified: a type the module exports is written by its bare name.

